        "body": "When client escalation rehearsal changes a record in the operational record, the operations lead approves and the preparer records the action; the two roles stay separate. A delegation needs a scope, a limit, an end date, and a named backup. Record rejected and deferred requests as well as approvals. If the team cannot reproduce the result from the record, the control is not finished. In this client escalation rehearsal case, the record points to the operational record and names the operations lead as the decision owner.\n\nName the owner for every open item, including items waiting on someone else. Record rejected and deferred requests as well as approvals. A complete packet is not an approval; the decision needs a person and a timestamp. The record should show the source, the decision, and the surviving value in one place. The client escalation rehearsal reviewer records the lessons record, the owner, and the next action in one place.\n\nApproval, payment, records disposition, and policy exceptions stay with the named owner. Name the owner for every open item, including items waiting on someone else. Do not let a deadline expand a role beyond its written limit. If two roles share a queue, mark which one decides each item. In the client escalation rehearsal queue, an item without a next action is treated as an exception."
